{"question": "A 21-year-old man comes to the emergency room with swelling and severe pain in his left lower leg that started 2 hours ago. He has no history of serious illness or trauma. His father has a history of pulmonary embolism. He has smoked one pack of cigarettes daily since he started college 3 years ago. He appears very distressed. He is 173 cm (5 ft 8 in) tall and weighs 92 kg (203 lb); BMI is 30.7 kg/m2. His temperature is 37\u00b0C (98.6\u00b0F), pulse is 94/min, respirations are 17/min, and blood pressure is 130/78 mm Hg. Physical examination shows a tender and mildly swollen left lower leg; dorsiflexion of the left foot causes severe pain in the calf. Laboratory studies show a platelet count of 184,000/mm3, a prothrombin time of 11 seconds, an activated partial thromboplastin time of 26 seconds, and positive fibrin split products. Ultrasonography of the left leg shows incompressibility of the popliteal vein with a hyperechoic mass and absent blood flow. The patient is administered a 5000 IU intravenous bolus of unfractionated heparin followed by a constant infusion. Six hours later, the activated partial thromboplastin time is 30 seconds. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms?", "options": {"A": "Hyperhomocysteinemia", "B": "Antithrombin III deficiency", "C": "Factor V Leiden", "D": "Protein C deficiency", "E": "Antiphospholipid syndrome"}, "pred_answer": "E", "gold_answer": "B", "question_domains": ["Emergency Medicine", "Cardiology", "Hematology", "Radiology", "Family Medicine"], "option_domains": ["Coagulation Disorders", "Thrombotic Disorders"], "question_analyses": {"Emergency Medicine": "The patient in this scenario is most likely suffering from a deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in his left lower leg, which is causing the swelling and severe pain. The key diagnostic findings that support this diagnosis include:\n\n1. **Clinical Presentation**: The patient has a history of sudden onset of swelling and severe pain in the left lower leg without any history of trauma. The pain is exacerbated by dorsiflexion of the foot, which is a classic sign of a DVT known as Homan's sign, although its diagnostic utility is debated. 2. **Physical Examination**: The examination reveals a tender and mildly swollen left lower leg, which is consistent with DVT. The presence of pain with dorsiflexion of the foot further supports this diagnosis. 3. **Laboratory Findings**: The laboratory results show a normal platelet count, normal prothrombin time, and a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) with positive fibrin split products. The prolonged aPTT and positive fibrin split products indicate the presence of a coagulation cascade activation, which is consistent with a thrombotic event. 4. **Imaging**: The ultrasonography of the left leg shows incompressibility of the popliteal vein with a hyperechoic mass and absent blood flow. This is a definitive sign of a DVT, as the vein should be compressible in the absence of thrombus. 5..", "Cardiology": "The patient in this scenario is most likely suffering from a deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in his left lower leg, which is causing the swelling and severe pain. The key diagnostic findings that support this diagnosis include:\n\n1. **Clinical Presentation**: The patient has a history of sudden onset of swelling and severe pain in the left lower leg, which is a hallmark of DVT. The pain is exacerbated by dorsiflexion of the foot, a sign known as Homan's sign, which is often associated with DVT. 2. **Physical Examination**: The examination reveals a tender and swollen left lower leg, which is consistent with DVT. The incompressibility of the popliteal vein and the absence of blood flow on ultrasonography are strong indicators of a thrombus obstructing the vein. 3. **Laboratory Findings**: The presence of positive fibrin split products suggests ongoing clot formation and degradation, which is typical in thrombotic conditions. The prolonged activated 